
QUICK START GUIDE TO CONFIGURE YOUR ENVIRONMENT AND BUILD WONDERGUI
===================================================================

WonderGUI uses CMake as its configuration and build system. Through CMake you
can easily create build- and project-files for your specific platform and 
development environment.

The base CMakeLists.txt is located in the WonderGUI directory.

CMakePresets.json in the WonderGUI directory has a configure preset for each
WonderApp framework on each platform. List those for your platform and
configure one of them from the WonderGUI directory:

cmake --list-presets
cmake --preset linux-wappsdl3gl
cmake --build build

The presets all build in build/: with Ninja on Linux (Debug, or Release with
the -release presets), with Visual Studio on Windows and with Xcode on macOS. Put presets of your own in CMakeUserPresets.json, which is not
checked in.

You can also run cmake yourself, picking the framework with -DWAPPLIB=<name>
(see the list in CMakeLists.txt) and the generator with -G.

The WonderGUI CMake files have been tested on Linux, Windows and OSX with 
several IDEs and development environments, see sections below for instructions
specific to your OS and development environments.


Running and debugging included applications
-------------------------------------------

Nearly all tools and example programs loads files from the resources/ directory 
and needs to be run from the WonderGUI directory to find them. Failure to find 
the resources-directory will result in an error message or crash.

So, in order to be able to run and debug these programs from within your IDE
you need to specify your debugger working path to the WonderGUI directory. 


*********************************************
**** SATISFYING DEPENDENCIES FOR YOUR OS ****
*********************************************

WonderGUI and everything related requires C++17 or higher.

Images are loaded with stb_image, which is included in thirdparty/ together
with tinyfiledialogs, so neither needs installing.

The WonderGUI base library (wondergui.lib) doesn't have any external 
dependencies, but separate addon libraries, example files and tools do.
There is a list of all build targets and their dependencies at the end of this
document.

Depending on your OS we recommend the following approaches to satisfy all
dependencies:


Linux
=====

All dependencies should be satisfied by your package manager. Just search,
download and install the following packages:

libsdl3-dev
libfreetype6-dev
libglew-dev

The SDL2 based WonderApp frameworks also need libsdl2-dev.


Windows
=======

Nothing to install. CMake downloads and builds SDL3, FreeType and GLEW when
it doesn't find them (option WONDERGUI_FETCH_DEPS, on by default). SDL2 is
only downloaded if one of the SDL2 based WonderApp frameworks is selected.


Mac OSX
=======

Nothing to install. CMake downloads and builds SDL3 and FreeType when it
doesn't find them (option WONDERGUI_FETCH_DEPS, on by default). SDL2 is only
downloaded if one of the SDL2 based WonderApp frameworks is selected.


**************************************************
**** CONFIGURING YOUR DEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT ****
**************************************************

Below is a list of development environments that are maintaned in and
tested with our CMake scripts. But since we are using CMake, any environment
decently supported by CMake should work decently. 


Visual Studio (not VSCode)
==========================

Visual Studio can be used in two ways:

1. You generate WonderGUI.sln using CMake and use that project, for example
   with "cmake --preset windows-wappwin32dx12", which puts it in build/.

2. You open the WonderGUI directory in Visual Studio. Visual Studio will
   notice it is a CMake project with presets and set it up as such.

I recommend you use #1, since that will setup your debugger working directory
correctly so that you can run (and debug) the example files and tools from
Visual Studio. Both ways works for just building stuff though.



Visual Studio Code (VSCode)
===========================

Have the CMake Tools and C/C++ extensions installed.

Open the WonderGUI directory in Visual Studio Code and pick a configure preset
when CMake Tools asks for one (or with "CMake: Select Configure Preset").
Building works from then on.

To run and debug, pick the tool or example in the status bar as the launch
target. CMake Tools' own debug and run buttons need the working directory set
in .vscode/settings.json:

    "cmake.debugConfig": { "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}" }

For F5, a single configuration in .vscode/launch.json covers all of them, with
"program" set to "${command:cmake.launchTargetPath}" and "cwd" to
"${workspaceFolder}". The .vscode directory is not checked in.


Linux Console (Makefiles, Ninja etc.)
=====================================

Configure with one of the linux- presets, see the top of this file, and build
with "cmake --build build" or "ninja -C build". Keep in mind that most tools and example files
needs to be run from the WonderGUI directory since they need files in the
resources/ directory. Failing to find them, some might print an understandable
error message while others will just crash.


XCode
=====

Download, install and use CMake to generate the XCode project file. The quickest
way is one of the macos- presets from the WonderGUI directory, for example
"cmake --preset macos-wappcocoametal", which puts the project in WonderGUI/build.

Or with the CMake GUI:

1. Make sure to satisfy the dependencies as described above.
2. Click "Browse Source..." and navigate to WonderGUI.
3. Create a directory named "build" in WonderGUI/. Click "Browse Build..." and
   select it.
4. Click Configure, select Xcode as generator for this project and make sure 
   everything goes through well. The table in the the middle of the window 
   gets some text with red background. This is normal and only CMakes way to 
   say that these values have been updated.
5. Click Generate.

You should now have a fully working XCode project in WonderGUI/build. 


********************************
**** ADDITIONAL INFORMATION ****
********************************

WonderGUI directory structure
=============================
		
resources/
        Various files such as fonts and bitmaps used by the included
        examples and tools.

scripts/
        Various scripts used for WonderGUI development. Undocumented
        and useless for you unless you intend to hack on WonderGUI.
		
wax/
        The Wax graphics library. Its own sources are in source/, its
        C API in c-api/ and its rendering backends and other extensions
        in extensions/.

wonderapp/
        The WonderApp framework, a simple application framework built on
        WonderGUI. Its sources are in source/, with one folder per
        platform (sdl2/, sdl3/, win32/, cocoa/), next to the tools and examples
        (tools/, examples/) that are built on it.

wondergui/
        The WonderGUI library. Its own sources are in source/, next to
        its extensions, tools, examples, widget kits and documentation
        (doc/).



Build targets and their dependencies
====================================


wax             - The Wax graphics library, containing the object model
                  and all low-level graphics. No external dependencies.

wondergui       - The WonderGUI base library, containing the framework
                  and all widgets. Requires wax, no external dependencies.

waxfreetypefont - Addon library for vector fonts using FreeType.
                  Requires FreeType to build.

waxopenglgfx    - Addon library for OpenGL rendering of UI.
                  Requires OpenGL and GLEW.

waxsoftgfx      - Addon library for generic software rendering of UI.
                  No external dependencies, but kept separately since
                  it is a fairly big library.

waxmetalgfx     - Addon library for rendering UI using Apples Metal API.
                  No external dependencies, except a Mac of course!

waxdx12gfx      - Addon library for rendering UI using DirectX 12.
                  No external dependencies, except Windows of course!

waxgfxstream    - Addon library for encoding drawing commands into a
                  binary stream for storage or playback on another device.
                  No external dependencies.

example01      - Minimal example program.
                  Requires SDL (SimpleDirectMediaLayer, see libsdl.org)

workbench       - For trying things out. Tests are picked from a list
                  and each one lives in its own file in
                  wondergui/tools/workbench/tests, see workbench.h.
                  Software rendered. Requires SDL3 and FreeType.

gfxdevicetester - Compares the native graphics device of the WonderApp
                  framework against the software reference: side by side
                  with a diff, accuracy of all tests and performance of the
                  native device.

unittester      - Simple console application to run some simple unit tests
                  on parts of WonderGUI. Requires FreeType.

